{"kind":"report","text":"Approved as an R1 design-adoption input at exact 05244cfbcd2619c877cbcfcac5985b47651bb31a, under live complete request 4752a1bc01dac489908c38fa2c3329c185aec7b6 and builder promise 6fd9a149545c1334ad0df6f8a3f1270855719e98; invitation 31b2ae7c and checker promise a55a52da. Primary de9888cd comes first, with companion corrections 0692ce1d and admission inventory 553bd84d. No blocking finding remains in this exact base-contract scope. This is an independent evidence-only review, not a revived v6 verdict, source implementation approval, sibling adoption or proof that the complete application is implemented.\n\nArchitecture. The reusable model gives each scope/incarnation its own locally admitted sealed history, fixed first-version placement, canonical references, explicit causal creation and source-owned relationship delivery. Exact selected facts remain immutable; mutable foreign state supplies no implicit authority. One snapshot/commit protocol covers acts, deliveries, outcomes, diagnoses, timed entries and checkpoints. Local dependency audit includes absence, range, time and freshness; whole-head validation is retained until narrower dependency validation earns equivalent coverage. Child genesis derives its cause from the source fact, ordinal and message digest in a distinct byte domain, preventing separate identical sends from colliding. Relationship revisions and duplicate-send refusal preserve both delivery orders. Bounded reads and complete evaluation distinguish missing evidence from falsehood.\n\nRevision 7 closes the two previously nonblocking text issues. Main 463–481 makes the exact-budget boundary match the algorithm: N successful timed attempts may be followed by the waiting input only after step 3 finds no remaining due transition. N+1 due transitions ends busy with the final transition still due and N committed entries kept. A transition becoming due between drain and commit still stops the ordinary input at 433–446. At 1744–1748 the replay wording now distinguishes an unrecorded busy response or dropped selection from independently committed timed entries, which are retained and checked. The bounded drain still charges every selection, including dropped/head-stopped attempts; alarms share its budget. No ordinary input passes unapplied expiry and a selected timed input does not block itself.\n\nSecurity. Admission rechecks the snapshot head, due ordering, guards, effects and time in the commit. Timeful entries fail closed on a backward clock; time-free entries may be clamped only when no transition is due. Provisional genesis opens no timed item or hold; validation and replay enforce that rule. Authenticated sends/results preserve target incarnation, source decision and retry correlation. Missing receiver storage cannot establish nonexecution; unknown attempts and credentials survive reset until separately settled. Causal proofs distinguish source-runtime trust from independently replayed history. Complete Boolean witnesses establish only irreversible results; absence, exact count and a satisfied upper bound need complete evidence. These are design rules, not runtime or operational clock proof.\n\nSimplification. One scope protocol, four explicit reference classes, one causal receipt/replay scheme and one ordered drain avoid a repository-wide admission sequence. Busy/dropped-selection responses need no artificial history entries. The three-row budget witness clarifies behavior without adding another state machine. The contract's replacement/removal maps preserve useful invariants while replacing legacy contract/log/Room/client formats; no identity adapter, dual model or pre-change reader is required. The admission inventory is a census and bounded source assessment, not a claim that every module or test has completed semantic review.\n\nOpen interfaces remain owned. Numeric budgets, retained-final-item bounds and alarm retry policy remain proposals for R4. Section 13 explicitly leaves total 